-- erwartet: N390
-- **1051 -- the second malformed guard: a CALL inside `Held(…)`.**
--
-- `beispiele/gift/1050` is the indexed place; this is the other way a guard can
-- fail to name a lock, and it fails DIFFERENTLY inside the checker, which is the
-- reason it has a probe of its own.
--
-- * `Held(SPERRE[eimer])` leaves a phantom behind: `held_aus_expr` takes
--   `Ort::text()` of the argument and pushes `SPERRE[...]` into the held-lock set,
--   which `N240` then prints as if it were a lock.
-- * `Held(waehle(eimer))` leaves NOTHING behind: the argument is not an `Ort` at
--   all, the `if let` in `held_aus_expr` does not fire, and the guard is simply
--   gone. Measured 2026-09-16 without the rule: `6 items, 1 errors, 1 hints` --
--   one `H007` ("`Verbindung.slots[...].zustand` is protected by `SPERRE`, and
--   `suche` does not hold it") and one `H008`. A refusal with the RIGHT verdict
--   and the WRONG reason: the author wrote a guard, and the report says none was
--   written.
--
-- So the two shapes are two failure modes of one hole, and the rule closes both
-- at the place where the words stand. `N390` is not `allein` here: `H007` speaks
-- too, which is exactly why the probe is worth keeping -- it pins that the new
-- refusal names the CAUSE beside the old one that names the symptom. With the
-- rule the file reads `6 items, 2 errors, 1 hints`: `N390` at the guard, `H007`
-- at the access, `H008` at the lock.

module gift::held_ueber_einen_ruf {

const ANZAHL : u32 = 16;

table Verbindung count ANZAHL {
    slot {
        zustand : u32 in 0 .. 3,
    }
}

lock SPERRE protects { Verbindung } rank 0 held <= 64 ops;

spec fn waehle(eimer : u32 in 0 .. 3) -> u32 in 0 .. 3
    effects { pure }
    = eimer;

impl fn suche(eimer : u32 in 0 .. 3, s : index into Verbindung) -> u32 in 0 .. 3
    requires Held(waehle(eimer))
    effects { reads Verbindung.slots }
    costs   <= 8 ops
{
    return Verbindung.slots[s].zustand;
}

}
